[color=#b45f06][size=4][b]PIXL RENDERER[/b][/size][/color]
[color=#999999][i]A modern DX11 shader suite for Skyrim SE[/i][/color]

[img width=552]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1704/images/189836/189836-1789150516-2066057463.jpg[/img]
[line]
[color=#a61c00][b]PLEASE READ[/b]
This is my first Skyrim mod project, built with substantial AI assistance, experimentation and hands-on testing. It has been tested in my own setup, including Skyrim SE 1.5.97 and the current GOG Skyrim Anniversary Edition 1.6.1170, but not across every hardware and mod combination. Unexpected issues are possible, so please back up anything important before trying PIXL Renderer.[/color]

If you prefer an established renderer with broader community testing, please check out [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/86492]Community Shaders[/url].

[line]
[b]PIXL Renderer[/b] started as a custom Community Shaders fork I made during my last Skyrim playthrough and then got slightly out of hand lol.

The idea was pretty simple: take the Skyrim I already like and push the rendering much further without completely changing its identity or requiring a million mods.

It brings lighting, materials, weather, terrain, characters, water, vegetation, snow, mud, atmosphere, reconstruction and camera processing together into one renderer rather than treating everything as a pile of unrelated effects.

I'm from a music production background, with some game development thrown into the mix. This is my first proper Skyrim mod project. A lot of it has been me learning, experimenting and having fun trying things out.

Install it, complete Quick Setup, and play Skyrim.

And yes, there are lots of sliders if you're that kind of person.

[line]
[img width=379]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1704/images/189836/189836-1787916499-2083474567.png[/img]

[line]
[size=4][b]WHAT DOES IT ACTUALLY DO?[/b][/size]

The short version:

[list]
[*]Hybrid global illumination and improved ambient lighting[/*]
[*]Expanded dynamic lights[/*]
[*]Improved sky, atmosphere, fog, volumetrics and cloud shadows[/*]
[*]Modernised materials, PBR, parallax and terrain rendering[/*]
[*]Improved skin, hair and thin-material shading[/*]
[*]Better grass and vegetation lighting[/*]
[*]World-space rain, wetness, puddles, splashes and runoff[/*]
[*]Snow and mud deformation, with character surface accumulation[/*]
[*]Improved water and underwater rendering[/*]
[*]Fake depth and movement behind supported windows[/*]
[*]DLSS / DLAA / FSR reconstruction paths[/*]
[*]Optional experimental Neural Rendering and frame generation[/*]
[*]HDR, exposure, camera processing, Photo Mode and capture tools[/*]
[*]Fast / Balanced / Enhanced / Cinematic quality presets[/*]
[*]A completely unnecessary number of advanced controls[/*]
[/list]

There are more individual systems under the hood, but you really don't need to know about all of them to use PIXL.

[size=4][b]THE GOAL[/b][/size]

Skyrim should still look like Skyrim.

Just with better lighting, better materials, better atmosphere and considerably fewer moments where something looks like it was rendered in 2011.

